#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# root

#% text
# `root(x => p(x), k)` stands for the `k`-th root of the polynomial `p`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The roots are ordered: the real roots ascending, then the complex roots by real part and then imaginary part.

#% item
# A `root` object is exact: it stays as it is and `n` evaluates it to any precision. `roots` and `solve` give `root` objects for factors without a solution in radicals.

#% item
# The canonical form: a root object prints with its polynomial as a slot function, `root(&(p(&1)), k)`, whatever variable it was written with; the polynomial is the primitive irreducible factor over the rationals that the root belongs to, and `k` its index among that factor's roots. A root of a factor of degree one is that rational number, of degree two the radical `(-b +- sqrt(b^2 - 4 a c))/(2 a)`.

#% item
# `root(f, k, 0)` is the same as `root(f, k)`.

#% item
# `root([f, x0])` stands for the root of an analytic function `f` (not a polynomial) that Newton's iteration from `x0` reaches, real or complex. The object exists only when that root is simple and isolated with proof (Krawczyk's test on enclosures of `f` and `f'` over a box): a double root, no root nearby, or a pole or branch cut in the way leave the call unevaluated.

#% item
# The canonical form of `root([f, x0])`: the factor of the numerator of `together(f)` that vanishes at the root, expanded, with coprime integer coefficients, multiplied by the unit (1, -1, or for complex coefficients also im, -im) that gives the shortest printed form without a leading minus sign, as a slot function; `x0` the machine number nearest the root. `n` refines the proved box to any precision, comparisons go through `n`, and arithmetic keeps the object.

#% item
# `solve`, `solve_values`, `reduce` and `n_solve` of one analytic equation with conditions on `re(x)`, `im_part(x)` or `abs(x - c)` give every root in that region as such objects (counted by the argument principle, each isolated); `maximize`, `minimize`, `max_value`, `min_value`, `arg_max` and `arg_min` on a closed bounded interval give critical points as such objects, the extremum proved by enclosures.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
root(x => x^3 - 2, 1)
#% output
#= root(&(-2 + &1^3), 1)

#% cell
n(root(x => x^3 - 2, 1))
#% output
#= 1.2599210498948732

#% cell
n(root(x => x^5 - x + 1, 1), 30)
#% output
#= -1.1673039782614186842560458998548422

#% cell
n(root(x => x^5 - x + 1, 2))
#% output
#= -0.18123244446987535 - 1.0839541013177107im

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The roots of a quintic, in order:

#% cell
n(table(root(x => x^5 - 3*x + 1, k), k, 5))
#% output
#= [-1.3887919844072543, 0.3347341419433527, 1.2146480426984618, -0.08029510011728015 - 1.328355109820654im, -0.08029510011728015 + 1.328355109820654im]

#% text
# Roots of reducible polynomials and of quadratics:

#% cell
root(&(&1^5 - 3*&1), 2)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
root(&(&1^2 - 3*&1 - 1), 2)
#% output
#= (1/2)*(3 + 13^(1/2))

#% cell
root(&(&1^5 - 3*&1), 3)
#% output
#= root(&(-3 + &1^4), 2)

#% text
# A root of a function that is not a polynomial, from a starting value:

#% cell
root([x => x - 2*sin(x), 1.9])
#% output
#= root([&(&1 - 2*sin(&1)), 1.895494267033981])

#% cell
n(root([x => x - 2*sin(x), 1.9]), 40)
#% output
#= 1.89549426703398094714403573809360169175134663

#% text
# A complex root, and a root that is not simple, which stays:

#% cell
root([&(exp(&1) - 1 - im), 0.3 + 0.8im])
#% output
#= root([&(1 + im - exp(&1)), 0.34657359027997264 + 0.7853981633974483im])

#% cell
root([&((&1 - 1)^2*exp(&1)), 1.1])
#% output
#= root([&((&1 - 1)^2*exp(&1)), 1.1])

#% text
# Every root in a region, and a maximum on an interval:

#% cell
solve(sin(x) - x == 2 and abs(x) < 5, x)
#% output
#= [[x -> root([&(2 + &1 - sin(&1)), -2.554195952837043])], [x -> root([&(2 + &1 - sin(&1)), 0.970288144203847 - 1.9542472384221157im])], [x -> root([&(2 + &1 - sin(&1)), 0.970288144203847 + 1.9542472384221157im])]]

#% cell
arg_min([gamma(x) + x, 1/2 <= x and x <= 3], x)
#% output
#= root([&(1 + digamma(&1)*gamma(&1)), 0.8349710458390793])

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The value is a root:

#% cell
r = n(root(x => x^5 - x + 1, 1)); r^5 - r + 1
#% output
#= -6.661338147750939e-16

#% text
# `solve` returns `root` objects:

#% cell
solve(x^5 - x + 1 == 0, x)[1]
#% output
#= [x -> root(&(1 - &1 + &1^5), 1)]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `roots`, `solve`, `n`, `find_root`
